/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Fiber at 1550 nm loses about 0.2 dB per km, so an 80 km span loses 16 dB and eight spans 128 dB, far beyond any power budget.
   - G = 10 log₁₀(Pout/Pin) dB
   - G is the gain; Pout and Pin are the signal powers out and in, in the same unit.
   - Five numbers appear on every amplifier data sheet.
   - A semiconductor optical amplifier (SOA) is a laser diode chip driven by current but stopped from lasing.
   - Two weaknesses limit its use.
   - An EDFA is a length of silica fiber, typically 10 to 30 m, whose core is doped with Er³⁺ ions.
   - The parts in signal order are an input isolator, a WDM coupler that merges pump and signal, the doped fiber, an output isolator and a gain-flattening filter.
   - Energy conservation caps the output.
   - PCE = (Ps,out − Ps,in) / Pp
*/
